<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Bouillon</h1><p class="st-meta">1456 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Bouillon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ardennes duchy whose castle Godfrey of Bouillon pawned for the First Crusade, later a small sovereign state of the La Marck and La Tour d'Auvergne families.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> pledged his castle and lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a> in 1096 to fund the First Crusade, and the bishops held Bouillon for centuries. From the mid-15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">La Marck family</a> gained control of it, and by the 1480s they ruled it in practice as an independent duchy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bouillon was a forested, sparsely populated land around a great castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semois">Semois</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-de-la-marck">Robert II de la Marck</a> and his heirs, also princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-sedan">Sedan</a>, styled themselves dukes. Possession was contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">La Marcks</a>, their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour d'Auvergne</a> heirs and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>. In 1678 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took Bouillon and gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godefroy-maurice-de-la-tour-dauvergne">Godefroy-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne</a> under French protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French troops annexed the duchy in 1795. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> it went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg</a> in 1815 and to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium</a> in 1839.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-bouillon.jpg" alt="Duchy of Bouillon" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Rössel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bouillon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1456 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bouillon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert II de la Marck, Godefroy-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bouillon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1626&amp;focus=duchy-of-bouillon" aria-label="Duchy of Bouillon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4796 444 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Bouillon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5116 700L5127 676M5116 700L5054 728M5116 700L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5127 676h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5116 700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5116 700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bouillon. <a href="/atlas?year=1626&amp;focus=duchy-of-bouillon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

